SM(589,{"n":"Hebrews 1.1-4","a":"Fr Stephen De Young Archives/Hebrews 1.1-4.mp3","d":2398.3,"s":[[0.16,"Come and study Holy Scriptures with us as Father Stephen DeYoung teaches verse by verse on the podcast The Whole Counsel of God."],[8.08,"Father Stephen is a former Protestant pastor and Bible teacher in the Reformed Church, but now an Orthodox priest serving at Archangel Gabriel Orthodox Church in Lafayette, Louisiana."],[21.76,"Okay, so we will go ahead and get started."],[25.36,"And when we get started in just a moment, we'll be starting St."],[29.92,"Paul's Epistle to the Hebrews."],[32.16,"Last time was just the big long introduction, which I'm not going to now repeat, because then we would just keep doing that over and over again and never get to the actual book."],[44.32,"But uh by the time people are hearing this, uh it the recording of that will also be uh available uh to go back and uh listen to."],[58.4,"Um the one thing that's very pertinent is, as I mentioned last time, not really an epistle in the same sense as the epistles of St."],[68.76,"Paul that we've been reading so far."],[70.68,"Um and the reason that's relevant tonight is that when we pick up here in Hebrews 1, verse 1, uh we're just gonna drop right into it."],[81.1,"So we don't have the sort of usual introductory material that we've seen that all of St."],[87.66,"Paul's epistles tend to have."],[90.64,"Where he identifies himself, you know, Paul, an apostle to da-da-da-da-da, etc."],[96.08,"etc. etc. All that's not here."],[98.0,"We just go straight uh straight into it."],[101.68,"Um the other thing I'll reiterate, because again, this is also pertinent, is we talked about how genre-wise, uh part of the reason for that is that this isn't really a letter."],[115.48,"This is more a written-down homily or sermon uh that's being sent to someone."],[124.28,"That's why we have some of the things you'd see at the end of a letter at the end, but we don't have the things at the beginning."],[130.76,"Um so um in in particular, I said last time, I argued last time, that uh the epistle as a whole is really sort of a commentary, a sermon, a homily on Psalm 11100. 9 in the Greek numbering"],[153.36,"So if you're looking up in an Orthodox Study Bible, and we're gonna see St."],[158.4,"Paul is gonna sort of work his way through that Psalm and quote it throughout."],[163.52,"And he's also going to, in the way that we is typical at this time, first century BC, first century AD, in Jewish circles when they're when they're doing this kind of thing, he'll also then bring in other passages of scripture, to sort of support their interpretation and the point that they're making about the interpretation of the main passage in question."],[192.27,"This is this is super common."],[193.31,"Um if you look at a collection of the Dead Sea Scrolls, you see this all over the place"],[199.75,"That's where they'll go like they'll do a verse, and then they'll start describing it, and then you'll get, you know, 23 other passages of scripture all interlaced in the interpretation."],[210.41,"Showing out connects to everything."],[212.17,"And then you get verse two."],[213.91,"Um, and it sort of works through that way."],[216.31,"Um, and so uh we're gonna see there are gonna be a bunch of different sort of subtopics and applications of things as we work our way through sort of as the interpretation goes."],[234.28,"Uh so the the sort of outline of it is very different than the outline of one of St."],[237.0,"Like St Paul's epistles."],[240.48,"It's not just like a straight argument."],[243.73,"Paul's Epistles, as we said he he seeds his themes into the opening, and then he kind of develops each of those themes in turn."],[251.33,"You could outline it, you know, if you wanted to with bullet points."],[254.61,"You can't really do that with Hebrews, because again, he's sort of working his way through and interpreting this psalm."],[261.43,"So this the outline or the structure is more the structure of the psalm than some kind of logical."],[269.19,"Here's my three points about this, and then three subpoints about that."],[273.83,"that. So all said, and I think it'll be clear as we get into it"],[274.59,"Um. Um unless there are any other um questions, comments, late breaking news, interesting tweets somebody wants to read."],[298.35,"Uh we'll go ahead and start in St."],[301.55,"Paul's Epistle to the Hebrews, chapter one, verse one."],[308.35,"God, who at various times and in various ways, spoke in time past to the fathers by the prophets, has in these last days spoken to us by his son, whom he has appointed heir of all things. through whom also he made the worlds"],[331.91,"Which is a great interesting translation."],[334.55,"We'll pause there, even though the sentence isn't over."],[336.95,"That's um this is one of those nice Greek sentences that goes on for page and a half."],[343.35,"But um we'll cut it here."],[346.39,"So we start with God, and when we see just God in this way, in uh the New Testament, and especially in St."],[358.15,"Paul, we're talking about God the Father."],[360.41,"As becomes obvious when he speaks about his son."],[363.87,"So God at various times and in various ways spoke in time past to the fathers by the prophets."],[373.27,"So the fathers here are the fathers of the faith."],[379.45,"It's all these Old Testament folks."],[384.06,"All of the ancestors in faith."],[386.3,"Um we know that St."],[391.18,"Paul, for when we were going through 1 Corinthians, uh is not primarily thinking, well, nobody at this time of history is thinking genetically, but even genealogically per se, in that strict way."],[404.11,"Uh this is this is fathers of the faith, our forebears."],[408.24,"And he spoke to them in the past by the prophets."],[413.54,"But notice he doesn't say he spoke to them in various ways, including the prophets."],[420.28,"The prophets are not one of the ways."],[422.69,"There are various ways through the prophets."],[427.57,"So the prophet, by definition, here, is the one through whom God is communicating."],[435.41,"So what are the different ways that prophets communicated?"],[437.81,"Well, we know, well, first of all, there's different words for prophet in the Old Testament, but that includes people who see visions."],[444.61,"That includes people who wrote."],[447.35,"In the in the traditional Jewish naming, we tend to name the Old Testament books in English by genre."],[455.05,"So they'll talk about like historical books."],[458.26,"And prophetic books and wisdom literature and that kind of thing."],[463.14,"But in the um traditional Jewish naming, the Hebrew naming, uh all those historical books, Joshua, Judges, Ruth, first and second Samuel, first and second Kings, or first through fourth kingdoms, all that stuff, is uh considered prophets."],[482.18,"It's usually called the former prophets."],[484.66,"And then the prophetic literature they would call the latter prophets."],[488.82,"Yes, sir. I have a question because if those historical books are included, the the prophets are almost all the people we know of. in the old testament"],[501.5,"So who are the fathers if you know who I mean David counts as a prophet under this."],[509.56,"And and so and then the common people who are being ruled over by David would be the fathers."],[516.04,"Okay. So fathers in the faith doesn't it's not the way we use church fathers."],[521.48,"Okay. It doesn't mean the the big guys."],[523.41,"It means forebears."],[524.69,"Yeah, okay. All all the people."],[526.45,"Forebears. So you have Moses the prophet and then you have the people."],[529.83,"Uh okay. Yeah."],[531.45,"And then that pattern continues."],[533.15,"So you have Joshua, even though we don't normally think of Joshua in terms of prophet per se."],[539.23,"He is one."],[540.19,"And the book of Joshua is one of the prophets."],[542.75,"And this naming is carried through"],[546.17,"When you when you look at like the saints' calendar in the Orthodox Church."],[550.42,"All those Old Testament figures are referred to as prophets."],[553.38,"Those sort of major ones."],[554.74,"The Old Testament saints are referred to as prophets."],[558.58,"You'll sometimes see the prophets and apostles, to cover the Old Testament and the New Testament."],[567.4,"That's why you see what we call the Old Testament referred to in the New Testament as the law and the prophets."],[574.36,"s That' including them."],[575.88,"Um That's not sort of skipping the historical books."],[581.78,"So yeah, so that's a way."],[585.31,"The way in which Joshua was a prophet is a little different than the way Moses was, and the way Samuel was, and the way Isaiah was."],[592.69,"But they were all the means through which God communicated to the people."],[599.17,"But there's always this sort of human intermediary."],[603.99,"And that human intermediary, that prophet, is a prophet in part because he has this direct communication with God."],[614.57,"That's part of what makes it a prophet."],[616.09,"Moses talks to God face to face."],[618.83,"David has this sort of right in the Psalms, etc."],[623.2,"Jeremiah, Isaiah. They have this more direct con.tact with God"],[629.24,"And then the rest of the people do it through that human intermediary."],[635.96,"So that is in the past."],[639.37,"And so, but verse 2, now he has in these last days spoken to us by his son."],[647.93,"Okay. So we'll reiterate last days."],[651.69,"Sometimes it's translated as latter days."],[654.59,"Don't confuse this with latter-day saints."],[656.59,"This isn't our Mormon friends."],[659.47,"But that's where they get it."],[663.47,"We talked before about how there's very clearly when you read the Old Testament prophets, this idea of a messianic age."],[671.71,"It's not just the Messiah's gonna show up and that's it"],[678.39,"The dead are all gonna rise and everybody's gonna be happy from then on out."],[682.84,"Then all the bad people are gonna get tossed, you know, and it's done."],[688.6,"And we talked about how Daniel 7 refers to this as a long time."],[693.58,"There's the enthronement of the Son of Man, and then there's a long time, and then the devil is judged."],[699.92,"And the wicked are judged."],[701.6,"Um appears all over the place, Isaiah, Ezekiel."],[706.4,"Um, and so the New Testament authors, as they come to understand that Jesus of Nazareth is the Christ, is the Messiah, is the Son of Man."],[719.5,"And they understand that his ascension was that enthronement from Daniel 7, they say, okay, well, now's the long time."],[728.8,"Now is this period of time."],[730.48,"St. John in Revelation is going to describe this as a thousand years."],[733.92,"It's this period."],[735.48,"Where um where Christ rules, but he rules in the midst of his enemies."],[740.92,"And that's part of Psalm 110."],[743.24,"Um that's uh the uh first verse of Psalm 110 is the Lord said to my Lord, which is literally in the the Hebrew, Yahweh said to my Lord, sit at my right hand until I make your enemies the footstool of your feet."],[757.72,"your Sit at my right hand, be enthroned, like we see in Daniel 7, until I make your enemies footstool."],[765.34,"So there's this period of time, when he's ruling amidst his enemies."],[769.12,"So when they talk about this being the last days, this being the latter days, and we're gonna see this as we get into the general epistles after Hebrew, after Hebrews, um a lot."],[780.88,"Say John is gonna say, Brethren, these are the last days."],[784.06,"Um that's what they mean."],[786.06,"They mean we're in this final time period before the end end, before the last judgment."],[791.5,"We've now entered into this period."],[793.9,"Uh that's as opposed to their fellow Jews who hadn't accepted Jesus as the Christ, as the Messiah."],[802.28,"Who are still looking forward to that period."],[804.52,"They still think the pre we're in the previous period."],[808.21,"So that's the point of identifying in the last days."],[810.85,"And so when he says here in these last days, he's spoken to us by a son, it's him speaking through the sun that has made that makes this the last days."],[820.15,"Now this transition has happened."],[822.31,"Now this is different."],[825.51,"Right off the bat, right off the bat, uh, he's just tacitly made the argument he's gonna be making through most of the first two chapters."],[837.67,"Which is uh if he's saying that there's now been this transition, there's now been this drastic change between the old epoch."],[845.27,"The the last age and this new epoch"],[851.61,"I won't call it a new age, because then people will freak out."],[854.89,"Uh these this last age, these last days."],[857.93,"There's been this big transition, then that means whoever he's calling the sun, and we of course know who that is, but whoever he's calling the sun can't be a human prophet."],[867.45,"Because then there'd be no change."],[870.99,"There'd be zero change."],[874.37,"So he's drawing a disjunction."],[876.93,"And if the disjunction is between there's a particular human person who has a direct communication with God, and then he passes that on to everyone else."],[892.18,"And now the sun is something different than that."],[896.58,"What does that imply?"],[898.82,"Because God is still speaking to us."],[900.76,"In both cases."],[902.84,"So if it's no longer through a a human intermediary, that means that the sun himself."],[911.1,"When we experience the sun, when the sun speaks, it's God himself speaking."],[916.24,"Because otherwise it wouldn't be different."],[920.1,"It wouldn't be different."],[921.22,"It'd be the same."],[922.83,"God talks to somebody and that person talks to us."],[926.59,"So he's already tacitly implied that."],[928.75,"Now he's going to be making this argument, like I said, through the first couple of chapters, but he's already implied it just with that, with this first transition."],[938.12,"This first contrast."],[941.8,"Then he says two things about the Son, whom he, God the Father, has appointed heir of all things."],[949.64,"So God the Father has appointed him the heir of all things."],[952.84,"Number one. And then number two, through whom also he made the worlds."],[959.56,"Which again is an interesting translation."],[964.2,"This is uh essentially uh in some cases, this is translated uh through whom he made the ages."],[977.66,"Um well, and then well, and it's the same word in in in and a lot of this is a western pre preface uh a western preference over against an eastern preference."],[989.4,"So the western preference, at least in in English, has been to translate this as worlds."],[997.72,"So you get like world without end, at the end of prayers and stuff."],[1003.27,"And you're like world without end, what is it."],[1005.81,"And we have unto ages of ages, usually in our in our English translation."],[1012.21,"They're translating the same thing."],[1016.19,"They're translating the same the same idea."],[1021.03,"Um how much do I want to play with Latin?"],[1025.03,"Um the the way that happens is that um the idea of the secularum doesn't mean what secular means now."],[1037.83,"St. Augustine in in City of God, um, when he's contrasting the city of God and the city of man."],[1046.49,"He's contrasting the kingdom of God and this world."],[1050.87,"He talks about how uh he he he kind of invests that word that we usually just translate world, secular, worldly."],[1059.2,"Secular. He invests that with the idea. that the kingdom of God, the city of God, is unchanging and eternal"],[1069.38,"And always the same."],[1071.22,"Remember, St. Augustine's a Platonist."],[1073.4,"So whereas the this world, the secular world, is always changing and in flux and sort of goes through these cycles."],[1082.52,"And he's writing, he's writing City of God at the time that the Western Roman Empire is collapsing."],[1088.45,"And he's kind of saying, hey, this is this is the way this world works."],[1091.49,"Empires rise, empires fall."],[1093.17,"Kingdoms rise, kingdoms fall."],[1095.01,"But sort of over above this is the kingdom of God, which is eternal and unchanging and lasts forever."],[1102.31,"And he's he's juxtaposing those things."],[1104.91,"And so the Western translations are influenced by that concept."],[1113.28,"So it becomes world without end, but what that's trying to indicate is an age, like an age of without end, as opposed to the ones of this world that do."],[1125.1,"And so that's how you get world without end versus ages of ages, which look nothing like each other."],[1130.16,"But are actually trying to get at the same sort of concept."],[1135.89,"So when this is saying that uh through Christ, God the Father created the Aeons, that's a way of saying through whom he created everything."],[1146.45,"Not just this age, the Messiatic Age, but all the previous ages, right"],[1146.47,"All the ages."],[1152.71,"The age before the flood."],[1154.25,"The age. Um everything."],[1159.55,"And so why does he list these two things?"],[1161.55,"Well, that thing is the one he lists second."],[1163.64,"But that's if everything, if all of the ages, if all of the creation is created by God the Father through the Son, then that means what?"],[1177.58,"He has to be eternal too."],[1179.9,"Because he's not part of any of those."],[1183.92,"He doesn't belong to any of those."],[1188.84,"And he wants to say that second because the first thing he says, he has appointed him the heir of all things."],[1195.96,"Well, appointed makes it sound like there's been some kind of change."],[1199.48,"And he just said there's been this transition in how God speaks."],[1204.92,"So he wants to."],[1206.68,"The part about the Father creating all the ages through the Son is to clarify that he's not saying, like, oh, that was plan A with the prophets, and now here's Plan B."],[1221.21,"Or these are two different things, or the Sun just appeared on the scene now."],[1224.83,"Or the Sun is part of this last age."],[1228.44,"So he's not part of it."],[1230.04,"But it's marked by the appearance of the Son through whom God speaks, through whom all things were made, and who is now the heir of all things."],[1243.62,"The heir of all things means he's going to inherit all things."],[1248.47,"So if he is the one through whom all things were created, and he's the heir of all things, that's kind of like saying he's the Alpha and the Omega."],[1259.99,"It's almost like the New Testament authors were all on the same page."],[1266.95,"Sorry, modern scholarship."],[1268.71,"Anyway. Well, this would have been written before the Gospel of John."],[1275.72,"Yes. And and St."],[1279.78,"John using that Alpha and Omega language is actually being drawn from the Greek translation of Isaiah."],[1285.54,"So I mean he's getting it from somewhere in the Old Testament, too."],[1288.42,"So yeah, none of the New Testament authors are making any of this up."],[1291.94,"They're right, they're connecting the dots."],[1294.43,"All the dots are there in the Old Testament."],[1296.83,"And now that they have personally encountered Christ as apostles, uh now they can see how the dots connect."],[1304.77,"Whereas before it was kind of hard to tell."],[1307.09,"You can connect dots in a lot of different ways."],[1310.05,"Um And so this idea of inheritance is important."],[1315.95,"this I hammered on, I know, a lot years ago when we were going through St."],[1323.27,"Paul's Epistle to the Romans."],[1325.27,"But um inheritance is the word that's used in the Torah and in especially uh Joshua."],[1341.59,"And then it's used less after that."],[1343.99,"It shows up in the prophets, but especially in that sort of earliest, what's sometimes called the hexate, if you throw in Joshua."],[1351.11,"Um, especially those books, inheritance is the word that's always used to refer to sort of all of the promises of God to Abraham."],[1361.89,"Which includes stuff about the land, but is definitely not limited to stuff about the land, and we're gonna get into that more in Hebrews later on."],[1368.93,"But uh all the promises about you know, your your descendants will become like the stars of heaven, all of those promises are referred to as the the inheritance."],[1382.18,"And so uh saying that the son is the heir of all things not only means he at the end, it's not just through him, but for him."],[1391.15,"Which is true, but it's going beyond that to connect to the whole idea of salvation as it was understood at the time with the son."],[1402.41,"Because we talked about we were going through St."],[1406.61,"Paul's epistle to the Romans that the heir, the firstborn."],[1411.3,"The firstborn son, the heir, uh received the entire inheritance."],[1417.3,"When the father died, the firstborn son got everything."],[1420.34,"It didn't get shared out, it didn't get nibbied up."],[1423.54,"Everything went to the heir to the firstborn son, and then he apportioned it out as he saw fit."],[1432.42,"We see this reflected a bunch of times in the Bible."],[1434.58,"Notably, there's a point where somebody comes to uh Christ as he's traveling and preaching and says, uh, tell my brother to divide the inheritance with me."],[1445.78,"Because apparently his brother had decided to just hold on to it for a while."],[1450.62,"Um, and uh Christ tells him, you know, don't worry about that, come follow me and be my disciple, let him have the money, you know, who cares."],[1459.58,"But um, but yeah, so that's that's how it worked."],[1464.36,"So when Christ is called the heir like this, it's not because God the Father is going to pass away and and right and Christ is gonna take over."],[1474.3,"It's heir as in he's the one who receives all those promises, and then he's the one who distributes those to his fellow heirs."],[1485.87,"To his brothers and sisters."],[1488.41,"So that's how the imagery is used in the New Testament."],[1490.97,"So saying he's the heir. is not just the for whom includes that"],[1496.05,"But also it means he's the one through whom the inheritance is going to be received by anyone who receives it."],[1504.99,"And that's why the word appointed there is used."],[1508.89,"That the birthright idea."],[1527.77,"So everything now is is through the Son."],[1531.37,"Indeed, as it has been from the beginning."],[1534.09,"Everything is through the sun so verse three who being the brightness of his glory and the express image of his person and upholding all things by the word of his power when he had by himself purged out our sins sat down at the right hand of the majesty on high okay we'll stop again because we're still in the same sentence folks um so uh first, right now, who is the son?"],[1573.05,"Part of this is why he's called the Son."],[1577.62,"Uh what is what does sonship mean?"],[1580.82,"Because he's just said all the ages were created through him."],[1584.34,"So if he's also eternal, why would he be called the Sun?"],[1590.85,"Why would you have a father-son relationship between two eternal persons?"],[1596.26,"Um he is the brightness of his glory."],[1604.42,"His is added, but in this case, I think correctly."],[1609.14,"That's that it's God's glory he's talking about."],[1611.3,"He's not just the brightness of glory in general, like he's shiny."],[1614.82,"Um, but he is the brightness of his glory and the express image of his person."],[1622.26,"So we'll start with. the brightness of his glory"],[1625.74,"What's that getting at there?"],[1627.26,"Well, there's the there are two different words in the Old Testament that both get translated glory."],[1635.34,"Not just in English and Greek."],[1637.34,"The word doksa gets used for both of them."],[1641.02,"One of them is kavod, which is the word that, for example, Moses uses when he asks to see God's glory."],[1651.5,"And God says, you can't see me and live."],[1654.46,"Okay. The word kavod, even though it's translated glory there, literally means like weight."],[1661.66,"Heaviness. Um so it's often used for ideas like substance or essence."],[1666.6,"And so. That we would use in English."],[1674.35,"The heavy, the weight, the mass."],[1677.37,"Sort of the um that's not the word that's in view here."],[1681.55,"The the other word is uh shekinah in Hebrew, which is glory, like the fiery cloud that fills the temple."],[1695.89,"Like that kind of glory."],[1697.79,"The way we usually think of glory."],[1699.59,"Like the nimbus, that's around a saint, in the halo, or Moses' face reflecting God's glory when he came down the mountain."],[1708.68,"That's the kind of glory that's in view here."],[1711.4,"Okay, so the brightness of his glory."],[1714.28,"So it's God's glory."],[1716.18,"And Christ is the brightness."],[1719.14,"What is that trying to imply?"],[1720.82,"Well, can you separate those two things?"],[1727.0,"Can you take glory and separate out the brightness?"],[1730.28,"You can talk about it separately."],[1734.37,"There's it's separable in speech."],[1736.45,"We could talk about those like they're two separate things, but they aren't two separate things."],[1743.78,"So this is one analogy that's being used."],[1749.0,"And you will sometimes see similar. analogies that are that are based off of this kind of thing in the church fathers when they're talking about the Holy Trinity"],[1758.54,"They'll talk about like light and heat and"],[1763.32,"And you can't separate right that they're all and they're all analogies."],[1767.88,"You don't want to press them, literally."],[1770.37,"But that has a biblical basis here."],[1773.11,"That that those kind of those kind of analogies, or a word and breath, and right that right, that you can't."],[1780.07,"Yes. I've done a different translation here, by standard."],[1784.87,"And it appears to be the same thing."],[1788.63,"There's the very stamp of his nature."],[1791.59,"That's the second part."],[1793.35,"That''ss that the express image."],[1793.75,"Yeah Okay. It was like yeah, that's their translation of this second part that we're about to talk about now, the express image of his person."],[1808.19,"And that's because the Greek word character is actually there and they're trying to keep it."],[1812.81,"And the idea there the idea that's being conveyed in the Greek, uh like what does express image mean?"],[1819.93,"Yeah, is that it's like if you have a signet ring, and you're making a stamp."],[1826.45,"Or a foot making a footprint."],[1831.61,"Um, that there's no difference."],[1835.93,"They're the same"],[1837.82,"The image on a seal and the image in the wax are the same."],[1841.08,"Um those are paired together."],[1845.08,"Why? Well, because that second image is of two separate things that match and are identical."],[1852.12,"And the first image is of two inseparable things."],[1858.81,"So again, just like we saw before, with who's appointed the heir, but through whom he created all things."],[1866.04,"We have the same kind of thing here."],[1868.38,"The father and the son are inseparably one, but also."],[1875.28,"The son is the image of the father."],[1877.28,"The exact image of the father."],[1882.06,"And it's worth noting that it's not vice versa."],[1888.46,"This isn't interchangeable."],[1889.98,"It's not the son is the image of the father, and the father is the image of the son."],[1894.72,"Those aren't interchangeable things."],[1896.41,"The son is the image of the father."],[1898.17,"Because the father is the father and the son is the son."],[1901.03,"Um, so we have to be clear about that, because uh in the West in general and in our contemporary times, all over the place, the doctrine of the Trinity has gotten really loose in terms of. how people talk about it and think about it"],[1923.39,"But the fathers are reading this, and this is where they're getting some of these things, is right here."],[1930.97,"And so again, these two these two points are made, not to say, oh well, this is this weird contradiction that no man can understand."],[1942.06,"But this is the approach that the ecumenical councils are going to take to these doctrines."],[1949.34,"Because on one hand, it's true, like, we can't comprehend the Holy Trinity as humans."],[1955.18,"We can't comprehend the mystery of Christ's incarnation as humans."],[1959.8,"But what you can do is you can sort of put a little fence around."],[1968.41,"You can put a guardrail around, okay, here's we know the truth is in here."],[1972.99,"So if if you go too far in this direction, you're you're gonna mess up."],[1978.37,"If you go too far the opposite direction, you're gonna get messed up."],[1981.65,"But if you go too far this way, you're gonna, you know, you get messed up, and too far the other way, you're gonna get messed up."],[1986.53,"So you can kind of."],[1987.67,"And so this is a way, and again, this is where they get it."],[1991.64,"In the um in the ecumenical councils, this way of speaking, this is an example of it here in the scriptures in Hebrews."],[1998.21,"So you say, on the one hand, inseparable, on the other hand, the son is the image of the Father."],[2007.83,"So you have these two things that are both true statements."],[2011.45,"So that you know the truth is in here."],[2014.07,"And if you go outside those parameters, no, no, no, no, no, no."],[2018.33,"You gotta come back, come back this way."],[2025.01,"And these these two aren't at the expense of each other."],[2032.29,"And upholding all things by the word of his power."],[2038.85,"Okay. Um. So the question here in interpretation, and I notice the Orthodox Study Bible leaves this super vague."],[2053.69,"Is who the his is."],[2060.57,"Who the his is."],[2062.25,"Is this saying that the son upholds all things by the word of his power?"],[2068.41,"Or is it saying the father upholds all things by the word of his power?"],[2073.77,"I'm gonna argue that it's saying the father does, because the son is the word of his power."],[2084.49,"So it's saying the father upholds things through the Son."],[2089.29,"We already had created."],[2090.67,"We already had Alpha, we already had Omega, now we've got the rest of the Son of the alphabet in between right also through Christ right so this is again reinforcing that idea everything that the father does he does through the son right when he had by himself purged our sins yeah, so that translation, the way we use by himself makes it sound like without any help."],[2130.84,"Like he did it all by himself."],[2133.14,"So is he now saying the son did this all by himself without the father?"],[2138.34,"Well, obviously not, because he just said the exact opposite."],[2142.63,"So the by, the word BY, that could be used a lot of different ways."],[2149.41,"I went by the store."],[2153.86,"Um I uh went there by car."],[2162.63,"Um so there's lots of different ways that that by can be can be used."],[2168.55,"Um and so here I'm gonna say that what what he's what it's saying is when he had by means of himself purged our sins."],[2183.71,"Um and that's as opposed to what by means of something else."],[2192.05,"Because when you have this language of purging of sin, this is language from the Day of Atonement ritual."],[2202.38,"Some people translate the word uh the the whole kefer verb family in Hebrew."],[2208.86,"Some people translate that now as purge."],[2211.66,"So they have the day of atonement, they call it the day of purgation."],[2216.08,"Because the the words mean to wipe or to smear or to right."],[2220.64,"And so this is something that's going to be developed."],[2226.6,"There's going to be a whole bunch about the Day of Atonement later."],[2229.62,"In the book of Hebrews."],[2231.27,"So Christ doesn't use something else or some other means."],[2237.05,"He doesn't go and grab two goats on the Day of Atonement and do the ritual at the temple."],[2243.94,"He's not part of the earthly priesthood."],[2246.52,"He he doesn't do that."],[2247.88,"He does it by means of himself."],[2252.46,"Through himself. And that's going to be expanded on as we go on."],[2257.5,"But uh after he had done this, he sat down at the right hand of the Majesty on high."],[2264.56,"Hand of the majesty on high, of course, is talking about the Father on high"],[2269.84,"So this is enthronement."],[2272.82,"You notice he sat down at the right hand."],[2275.86,"Remember Psalm 110 or 109, verse 1?"],[2279.3,"The Lord said to my Lord, Sit at my right hand until I make your enemies your footstool."],[2285.46,"And this is the enthronement of the Son of Man in Daniel 7."],[2288.99,"So after he completes this, he sits down, he is enthroned."],[2296.19,"At the right hand of the Father."],[2299.15,"Verse 4 having become so much better than the angels, as he has by inheritance obtained a more excellent name than they."],[2310.63,"So we're finally now at the end of the first sentence."],[2316.07,"The first paragraph and section here."],[2319.35,"So that language is important, and he's going to come back to it because it's a reference to in the Old Testament."],[2329.29,"Who is the son who is man that you're mindful of him, the son of man?"],[2332.57,"You made him a little lower than the angels, but crowned him with glory and honor."],[2337.37,"That's going to get interpreted later on."],[2339.86,"Um, so we are seeing some of the later themes seated in here."],[2343.74,"But not in really the same way St."],[2345.42,"Paul typically does it."],[2346.46,"But they are here."],[2347.7,"Um so he's now above right all the angels."],[2355.48,"As a human on earth, you're not right above the angels, but he's now above the angels, as he has by inheritance obtained a more excellent name than they."],[2367.56,"Um, and that's gonna be uh developed here too."],[2375.8,"Listen next time as Father Stephen DeYoung continues his study of the scriptures on the Whole Counsel of God."],[2383.24,"Father Stephen's email address is whole counsel at ancientfaith.com."],[2388.6,"That's whole counsel at ancientfaith.com."]],"o":[{"t":21.76,"lv":2,"h":"Recap: no intro, a homily on Psalm 109","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Starting <b>St. Paul's Epistle to the Hebrews</b> proper</li><li class=\"d0\">Last time = the big intro, won't repeat - <b>recording available</b> to go back to</li><li class=\"d0\">Pertinent point: <b>not an epistle</b> like Paul's others</li><li class=\"d1\">Hebrews 1:1 \"we're just gonna drop right into it\"</li><li class=\"d1\">none of the usual <b>\"Paul, an apostle to…\"</b> opening</li><li class=\"d0\">Genre: not really a letter - a <b>written-down homily or sermon</b> sent to someone</li><li class=\"d1\">hence things from a letter's <b>end</b> but not its beginning</li><li class=\"d0\">Argued: whole thing is a <b>commentary/sermon on Psalm 110 (109 Greek)</b></li><li class=\"d1\">check your <b>Orthodox Study Bible</b>; Paul works through the psalm and quotes it throughout</li></ul>"},{"t":153.36,"lv":3,"h":"Commentary method and structure","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Typical in <b>1st c. BC / 1st c. AD Jewish circles</b></li><li class=\"d1\">bring in <b>other passages of Scripture</b> to support the interpretation of the main passage</li><li class=\"d1\">\"super common\" - <b>Dead Sea Scrolls</b>: a verse, a description, then \"23 other passages interlaced\"</li><li class=\"d1\">shows it all <b>connects to everything</b>; then verse two…</li><li class=\"d0\">Many <b>subtopics and applications</b> as the interpretation goes</li><li class=\"d0\">Outline very different from Paul's epistles</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul: <b>seeds themes</b> in the opening, develops each in turn - could bullet-point it</li><li class=\"d1\">Hebrews: can't - structure is <b>the structure of the psalm</b>, not \"three points, three subpoints\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Any questions, comments, \"late breaking news, interesting tweets\"? Then start</li></ul>"},{"t":298.35,"lv":2,"h":"Hebrews 1:1 - God spoke by the prophets","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Hebrews 1:1-2</b>: \"God, who at various times and in various ways, spoke in time past to the fathers by the prophets, has in these last days spoken to us by his Son, whom he has appointed heir of all things, through whom also he made the worlds\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"a great interesting translation\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Pause mid-sentence - a Greek sentence that \"goes on for a page and a half\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Just <b>\"God\"</b> in NT, esp. Paul = <b>God the Father</b></li><li class=\"d1\">obvious when he speaks of <b>his Son</b></li></ul>"},{"t":346.39,"lv":2,"h":"The fathers = forebears","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Fathers</b> = <b>fathers of the faith</b>, all the OT ancestors in faith</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul not thinking <b>genetically</b>/genealogically (1 Corinthians) - \"our forebears\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Spoke to them <b>by the prophets</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not \"in various ways, <b>including</b> the prophets\"</li><li class=\"d1\">prophets are <b>not one of the ways</b> - various ways <b>through</b> the prophets</li><li class=\"d1\">prophet = <b>the one through whom God communicates</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Ways: different Hebrew words for prophet - visions, writing</li></ul>"},{"t":413.54,"lv":3,"h":"Former and latter prophets","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">English names OT books by <b>genre</b>: historical, prophetic, wisdom</li><li class=\"d0\">Traditional Jewish naming: <b>Joshua, Judges, Ruth, 1-2 Samuel, 1-2 Kings</b> (\"first through fourth kingdoms\") are <b>prophets</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>former prophets</b>; the prophetic literature = <b>latter prophets</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Carries through: <b>saints' calendar</b> calls OT figures <b>prophets</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"the prophets and apostles\" = OT and NT</li><li class=\"d1\">NT says <b>\"the law and the prophets\"</b> - includes the historical books</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Joshua</b> a prophet differently than <b>Moses, Samuel, Isaiah</b> - all <b>means of God's communication</b></li></ul>"},{"t":488.82,"lv":3,"h":"Q: who are the fathers if prophets are almost everyone?","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: if historical books count, prophets are almost everyone we know - <b>who are the fathers</b>?</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>David</b> counts as prophet → the common people ruled by David are the fathers</li><li class=\"d1\">Moses the prophet, then the people; <b>Joshua</b>, same pattern</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Fathers in the faith</b> ≠ church fathers, \"the big guys\"</li><li class=\"d1\">means <b>forebears</b>, all the people</li><li class=\"d0\">Always a <b>human intermediary</b></li><li class=\"d1\">prophet because of <b>direct communication with God</b>: Moses <b>face to face</b>, David in the Psalms, Jeremiah, Isaiah</li><li class=\"d1\">rest of the people hear it <b>through the intermediary</b></li></ul>"},{"t":639.37,"lv":2,"h":"1:2 - \"in these last days\"","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Verse 2: now <b>\"in these last days spoken to us by his Son\"</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Last days / latter days</b> - \"don't confuse with latter-day saints… our Mormon friends\"</li><li class=\"d0\">OT prophets: a <b>messianic age</b> - not just Messiah shows up and all dead rise, bad people tossed</li><li class=\"d1\">Daniel 7: <b>enthronement of the Son of Man</b>, then <b>a long time</b>, then devil and wicked judged</li><li class=\"d1\">also Isaiah, Ezekiel</li><li class=\"d0\">NT authors: Jesus = Christ = Son of Man; <b>ascension = Daniel 7 enthronement</b> → \"now's the long time\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>St. John</b>, Revelation: a <b>thousand years</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Christ <b>rules in the midst of his enemies</b> - Psalm 110</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Psalm 110:1</b>: \"Yahweh said to my Lord, sit at my right hand until I make your enemies the footstool of your feet\"</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Last days\" (general epistles; John: \"Brethren, these are the last days\") = <b>final period before the last judgment</b></li><li class=\"d1\">vs. fellow Jews who <b>haven't accepted Jesus</b>, still think they're in the previous period</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>speaking through the Son</b> is what makes it the last days</li></ul>"},{"t":825.51,"lv":2,"h":"Tacit argument: the Son is not a human prophet","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"Right off the bat\" - tacit argument he'll make through <b>most of chapters 1-2</b></li><li class=\"d0\">A drastic <b>transition between old epoch and new</b> - \"won't call it a new age, people will freak out\"</li><li class=\"d0\">So whoever the Son is, <b>can't be a human prophet</b></li><li class=\"d1\">else <b>zero change</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Disjunction: human with direct communication passes it on <b>vs.</b> the Son</li><li class=\"d1\">God still speaking to us in both cases</li><li class=\"d1\">no human intermediary → <b>when the Son speaks, God himself speaks</b></li><li class=\"d1\">otherwise \"it wouldn't be different\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Implied already by this <b>first contrast</b></li></ul>"},{"t":941.8,"lv":2,"h":"Heir of all things; through whom he made the worlds","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Two things about the Son:</li><li class=\"d0\">1. <b>Appointed heir of all things</b> - by God the Father</li><li class=\"d0\">2. <b>Through whom he made the worlds</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"interesting translation\" - in some, <b>\"made the ages\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\">same word; <b>Western vs. Eastern</b> preference in translating</li><li class=\"d1\">West (English): <b>\"world without end\"</b> in prayers vs. <b>\"unto ages of ages\"</b> - same thing</li></ul>"},{"t":1021.03,"lv":3,"h":"Worlds vs. ages - Latin and Augustine","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Latin <i>saeculum</i> doesn't mean what <b>\"secular\"</b> means now</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Augustine</b>, <i>City of God</i>: city of God vs. city of man; kingdom of God vs. this world</li><li class=\"d1\">invests \"world/secular\" with the idea: kingdom of God <b>unchanging, eternal, always the same</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Augustine = <b>Platonist</b>; this world <b>in flux, cycles</b></li><li class=\"d1\">writing as <b>Western Roman Empire collapsing</b>: \"empires rise, empires fall\"; above it the kingdom of God lasts forever</li><li class=\"d0\">Western translations influenced → <b>\"world without end\"</b> = an age without end, vs. ones of this world that do</li><li class=\"d1\">\"look nothing like each other\" but <b>same concept</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1135.89,"lv":3,"h":"Why list both: he must be eternal","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Through Christ, Father <b>created the Aeons</b> = through whom he created <b>everything</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not just the Messianic age but <b>all previous ages</b> - before the flood, etc.</li><li class=\"d0\">Why list these two? Second one matters:</li><li class=\"d1\">all creation made through the Son → he must be <b>eternal</b>, belongs to none of the ages</li><li class=\"d1\">\"appointed\" sounds like <b>a change</b>, and he just said there's a transition in how God speaks</li><li class=\"d1\">so: not <b>\"plan A with the prophets, now plan B\"</b>; not that the Son <b>just appeared</b> or is part of this last age</li><li class=\"d1\">he's <b>marked by his appearance</b> - the one through whom God speaks, all things were made, now <b>heir of all things</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Creator + heir of all = <b>Alpha and Omega</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"almost like NT authors were all on the same page. Sorry, modern scholarship\"</li><li class=\"d1\">written <b>before John's Gospel</b>; John's Alpha/Omega drawn from <b>Greek Isaiah</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"none of the NT authors are making any of this up\" - <b>connecting the dots</b> now that they've met Christ</li></ul>"},{"t":1310.05,"lv":3,"h":"Inheritance and the firstborn heir","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Inheritance</b> - hammered on years ago in <b>Romans</b></li><li class=\"d1\">word used in <b>Torah, esp. Joshua</b> (the hexateuch), less after; shows up in prophets</li><li class=\"d1\">= <b>all God's promises to Abraham</b> - land, but \"definitely not limited\" (more in Hebrews later)</li><li class=\"d1\">descendants like the <b>stars of heaven</b> - all called the inheritance</li><li class=\"d0\">Heir of all things: not only <b>through him but for him</b></li><li class=\"d1\">connects to <b>salvation</b> as then understood</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Firstborn son = heir</b> got the <b>entire inheritance</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not shared out; he apportioned it as he saw fit</li><li class=\"d1\">Gospels: \"tell my brother to divide the inheritance with me\" → Christ: \"come follow me… let him have the money\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Christ as heir ≠ Father passing away</li><li class=\"d1\">he <b>receives all the promises</b>, then <b>distributes to his fellow heirs</b>, \"brothers and sisters\"</li><li class=\"d1\">through him anyone receives it - that's why <b>\"appointed\"</b>: the <b>birthright</b> idea</li><li class=\"d0\">Everything through the Son, \"as it has been from the beginning\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1527.77,"lv":2,"h":"1:3 - the Son and sonship","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Hebrews 1:3</b>: \"who being the brightness of his glory and the express image of his person and upholding all things by the word of his power, when he had by himself purged our sins, sat down at the right hand of the Majesty on high\"</li><li class=\"d1\">still the same sentence - stop again</li><li class=\"d0\">Who is the Son? What does <b>sonship</b> mean?</li><li class=\"d1\">all ages created through him; if eternal, <b>why \"Son\"</b>?</li><li class=\"d1\">why a <b>father-son relationship between two eternal persons</b>?</li></ul>"},{"t":1596.26,"lv":3,"h":"Brightness of his glory","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>\"His\"</b> glory added, \"correctly\" - God's glory, not just shiny in general</li><li class=\"d0\">Two OT words both translated glory (Greek <i>doxa</i> for both)</li><li class=\"d1\">1. <i>kavod</i> - Moses asking to see God's glory, \"you can't see me and live\"</li><li class=\"d1\">literally <b>weight, heaviness</b> → substance, essence - <b>not in view here</b></li><li class=\"d1\">2. <i>shekinah</i> - fiery cloud filling the temple; the <b>nimbus/halo</b> around a saint; <b>Moses' face</b> reflecting God's glory</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Brightness of his glory</b>: can you separate glory from brightness?</li><li class=\"d1\">separable in speech, <b>not in fact</b> - one analogy</li><li class=\"d1\">church fathers on the Trinity: <b>light and heat</b>, <b>word and breath</b> - analogies, don't press literally, but have a <b>biblical basis here</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Q: different translation: \"the very stamp of his nature\"</li><li class=\"d1\">that's the second part - <b>express image</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Greek word <i>charakter</i> - they keep it</li></ul>"},{"t":1845.08,"lv":3,"h":"Express image: inseparable yet exact image","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Express image</b>: signet ring and stamp, a foot making a footprint</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>no difference</b> - image on the seal and image in the wax are the same</li><li class=\"d0\">Two images paired - why?</li><li class=\"d1\">second: two separate things that <b>match, identical</b></li><li class=\"d1\">first (brightness/glory): two things <b>inseparable</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Same as <b>heir / through whom he created</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Father and Son <b>inseparably one</b>, but also Son is the <b>exact image</b> of the Father</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Not interchangeable</b> - Son is image of Father, not vice versa</li><li class=\"d1\">\"Father is the Father and the Son is the Son\"</li><li class=\"d1\">West and our times: doctrine of the Trinity \"gotten really loose\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1901.03,"lv":3,"h":"Guardrails: how the councils speak","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Fathers read this - \"where they're getting some of these things\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Two points <b>not a weird contradiction no man can understand</b></li><li class=\"d1\">the <b>ecumenical councils'</b> approach</li><li class=\"d1\">can't comprehend the <b>Holy Trinity</b> or the <b>Incarnation</b></li><li class=\"d1\">but can <b>put a fence / guardrail</b> around: \"the truth is in here\"</li><li class=\"d1\">too far one way → mess up; too far the opposite → mess up</li><li class=\"d0\">Example here: <b>inseparable</b> on one hand, <b>Son is image of the Father</b> on the other</li><li class=\"d1\">both true → inside those parameters; outside → \"come back this way\"</li><li class=\"d1\">neither at the <b>expense of the other</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2032.29,"lv":2,"h":"Upholding all things by the word of his power","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>\"Upholding all things by the word of his power\"</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Question: <b>who is the \"his\"</b>? - Orthodox Study Bible \"leaves this super vague\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Son upholds by <b>his</b> word? or <b>Father</b> upholds by <b>his</b> word?</li><li class=\"d0\">He argues: the <b>Father</b>, because the <b>Son is the word of his power</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Father upholds things <b>through the Son</b></li><li class=\"d1\">already had <b>Alpha, Omega</b>, now the rest of the alphabet in between - <b>through Christ</b></li><li class=\"d1\">reinforces: <b>everything the Father does he does through the Son</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2130.84,"lv":3,"h":"Purged our sins by means of himself","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"When he had <b>by himself</b> purged our sins\" - sounds like <b>without any help</b></li><li class=\"d1\">did the Son do it without the Father? - obviously not, he just said the opposite</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>\"By\"</b> has many uses: \"went by the store,\" \"by car\"</li><li class=\"d0\">He reads: <b>by means of himself</b> - as opposed to by means of something else</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Purging of sin</b> = <b>Day of Atonement</b> language</li><li class=\"d1\">Hebrew <i>kafar</i> family; some translate <b>purge</b> - \"day of purgation\"</li><li class=\"d1\">words mean <b>to wipe or smear</b></li><li class=\"d1\">a whole bunch more on the Day of Atonement later in Hebrews</li><li class=\"d0\">Christ doesn't use another means</li><li class=\"d1\">no <b>two goats</b>, no ritual at the temple, <b>not part of the earthly priesthood</b></li><li class=\"d1\">does it <b>through himself</b> - expanded as we go</li></ul>"},{"t":2257.5,"lv":3,"h":"Sat down at the right hand of the Majesty","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"After he had done this, he <b>sat down at the right hand of the Majesty on high</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Majesty on high = <b>the Father</b></li><li class=\"d0\">= <b>enthronement</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Psalm 110/109:1</b> - \"The Lord said to my Lord, sit at my right hand until I make your enemies your footstool\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>enthronement of the Son of Man, Daniel 7</b></li><li class=\"d1\">after completing this he sits down, enthroned at the Father's right hand</li></ul>"},{"t":2299.15,"lv":2,"h":"1:4 - better than the angels","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Hebrews 1:4</b>: \"having become so much better than the angels, as he has by inheritance obtained a more excellent name than they\"</li><li class=\"d0\">End of the <b>first sentence / paragraph</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Language important - he'll come back to it</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Psalm 8</b>: \"Who is man that you're mindful of him, the son of man? You made him a little lower than the angels, but crowned him with glory and honor\"</li><li class=\"d1\">interpreted later - <b>later themes seeded</b> here, \"not in really the same way Paul typically does\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Now <b>above all the angels</b></li><li class=\"d1\">as a human on earth you're not above angels, but he is now</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>more excellent name</b> by inheritance - developed here too</li></ul>"}]});
